 want to include here  would be flowering plants in bird  it kind of makes sense that they evolve  around the same time because again birds  often pollinate flowers well this was  about a hundred and seventy five million  years ago on our 24-hour timeline that  puts them just after 11 p.m.  well let's add this let's add Homo  sapiens us humans fossils indicate that  humans evolved around 150 200,000 years  ago look at our scale one minute is  equivalent to three million years were  not even a minute old on this scale in  fact we're about three or four seconds  old on this timeline and when you look  at the pattern there's a really obvious  conclusion life began very simple but  has become gradually more complex over  the years well so let's talk about  fossils a little more you know there are  what are called transitional fossils  fossils that link to different types of  life you know here's a great example of  one for a long time scientist I  anticipated to birds well if that's true  shouldn't there be some fossils out  there that are part reptile and part  bird some transitional fossils and this  picture shows a transitional fossil  called Archaeopteryx an organism that  shares both bird and reptile  characteristics well in the fossil  picture you can see some of its reptile  features you can see the bony tail upon  closer examination it has teeth well  what about the bird features well it has  wings and it has feathers and it has a  wishbone and so here we have a good  example of a fossil with characteristics  of two different types of organisms  birds and reptiles you know for a long  time scientists have believed that some  kind of land mammal eventually led to  the evolution of whales well if that's  true shouldn't there be something out  there some fossils out there that's part  land  and partway land and here's a great  example of that basilosaurus it shares  both whale and land mammal features well  the land features would be the fact that  it has teeth more similar to a wolf and  it has hind limbs you know actual leg  bones but when it comes to the whale  features the ears are the bones of a  whale and as a long spine like a whale  and we found it we find these fossils in  dried-up ocean beds so we know it was a  it was an aquatic organism you know  another example for a long time  scientists have believed that fish  eventually evolved into amphibians well  that's true shouldn't they be out there  something out there that's part fish in  part and phibian well here's a great  example called Tiktaalik it's a fossil  the fossil remains of an organism that's  part fish and part amphibian well what's  so fish about it is you can see the  scales on its back and it has fins but  what's so amphibian about it is that it  has eyes on the top of its head like an  amphibian and it has little wrists like  an amphibian and so when you examine  these transitional fossils it it really  nicely demonstrates that for instance  birds and reptiles have a common  ancestor so these are great specimens  here that show that life is evolved over  the years well the next topic I want to  mention are what are called vestigial  structures these are organs or body  parts which have lost most or all of  their original function or their a  function is greatly reduced from what it  once was you know there's all kinds of  non-human examples for instance the eyes  of blind cave fish for instance the the  wings of flightless birds like emus and  ostriches or the leg Spurs of snakes  like this boa constrictor these are  vestigial remnants of the fact that  their ancestors  we had legs in the past well let's but  let's focus on human examples of  vestigial structures let's first talk  about as in human embryo the human  embryo has gill slits or pharyngeal  arches as they're called now they're  vestigial they don't help the embryo  breathe like gills would help a fish  breathe but the fact that human embryos  have gills is a pretty clear indication  of ou